---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import SoundCloudPlayer from '../components/SoundCloudPlayer.astro';
import { OPEN_CALL } from '../config';
import { CONTRIBUTORS } from '../lib/contributors';

const tallySrc = OPEN_CALL.tallyFormId
  ? `https://tally.so/embed/${OPEN_CALL.tallyFormId}?alignLeft=1&hideTitle=1&transparentBackground=1&dynamicHeight=1`
  : null;

const description =
  'Open call: send a recording, and it becomes part of the corpus a machine-listening system plays through. An album by Ömer Kurtuluş built from contributed sound.';

/** The recording brief. Every line here came out of building a corpus that worked. */
const BRIEF = [
  ['Format', 'WAV. 44.1 or 48 kHz, 24-bit preferred — 16-bit is fine. Mono or stereo, either works.'],
  ['Dry', 'No reverb, no compression, no EQ, no noise reduction. Nothing that cannot be undone.'],
  ['Level', 'Peak around −6 dBFS. Leave headroom; never clip.'],
  ['Room', 'The quietest one you have. Traffic and fans end up in the corpus too.'],
  [
    'Let it ring',
    'Do not trim the decay. The tail of a bell, a plate, a string is the most useful part of it — cut tails make a corpus that can only tick.',
  ],
  [
    'Leave silence',
    'A second or two between events. The system slices on attacks, and events that run together cannot be told apart.',
  ],
  [
    'Bowed and rubbed sounds',
    'A sound that fades up out of silence has no attack, and an attack is what the slicer looks for. Either start it decisively, or tell me in the form and I will handle it.',
  ],
  ['One per file', 'A separate file for each technique. 30 seconds to 3 minutes each.'],
  ['Naming', 'surname_source_technique.wav — e.g. kurtulus_cymbal_bowed.wav'],
];

/** The pipeline, in the four steps that actually constrain what a recording must be. */
const PIPELINE = [
  ['01', 'Cut', 'Your file is sliced at its attacks. Each slice is one usable fragment — roughly half a second to five.'],
  ['02', 'Analyse', 'Every slice is measured: pitch, pitch class, spectral shape. That measurement is the only way the system knows it exists.'],
  ['03', 'Navigate', 'A driving sound — an exciter — is fed in live. The system matches what it hears against the corpus and plays the closest slices, in an order the corpus never had.'],
  ['04', 'Fix', 'One continuous pass is recorded, then edited and mastered. That take becomes the track.'],
];

const FAQ = [
  [
    'Do I need to be a musician?',
    'No. Objects, surfaces, metal, wood, glass, paper, water, breath, voice — anything that sounds. Several of the most useful things in a corpus are not instruments.',
  ],
  [
    'Do I need to read music?',
    'No. What you get is a page of graphic notation: simple prompts, shapes and words. There is no staff to read.',
  ],
  [
    'Will I hear my own sound in the piece?',
    'Probably not as itself. The system does not play your recording back — it takes its shape apart and re-speaks it through the rest of the corpus. You may recognise a gesture rather than a sound.',
  ],
  [
    'Can I still use my recording elsewhere?',
    'Yes, anywhere, for anything. You keep the copyright. What you give me is permission, not ownership.',
  ],
  [
    'How much should I send?',
    'One good file is a real contribution. Five is generous. There is no minimum.',
  ],
];
---

<BaseLayout title={OPEN_CALL.title} description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    title="What the C*rp*s"
    eyebrow="Open call"
    lead="An album assembled from recordings sent by people I have mostly never met. Send a sound; it becomes part of the body the system speaks with."
  />

  <div class="shell">
    <div class="body grid">
      <div class="body__main">
        <section class="prose">
          <p>
            I am making an album called <em>What the C*rp*s</em>, and I would rather make it with
            people than with a sample library. This page is the invitation and the instructions.
          </p>

          <h2>What a corpus is</h2>
          <p>
            A corpus is a body of recorded sound that has been cut into slices and analysed one
            slice at a time — pitch, brightness, spectral shape. It is not a playlist and not a
            sample pack. It is the material a listening system is allowed to reach for.
          </p>
          <p>
            I work with <strong>Somax2</strong>, developed at IRCAM. It listens continuously to an
            incoming sound and, in real time, navigates the corpus looking for slices that match
            what it is hearing — then plays them, in an order the corpus never had. It does not
            reproduce your recording. It learns its shape and answers in other material.
          </p>
          <p>
            Which means the corpus is the instrument. Whatever goes into it is what the system is
            able to say. Right now I am collecting it.
          </p>

          <h2>Where it stands, and why I need you</h2>
          <p>
            The pieces below already exist. I built them to find out whether the idea holds, and
            their corpora were assembled from Creative Commons recordings on Freesound — a dripping
            tap, bowed crotales, a prepared piano someone else recorded years ago. They work. They
            are also, in a sense I cannot argue my way out of, made of other people's leftovers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That is the part I want to replace.</strong> The album is meant to be extended and
            remade with material recorded by actual people, for this, on purpose — not scavenged
            from an archive. A corpus built from strangers who agreed to be in it is a different
            object from one built from search results, and I think you can hear the difference.
            These tracks are the proof of concept. What comes next should be the real thing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="listen">
          <h2 id="listen" class="section__title">Listen</h2>
          <p class="section__intro">
            The Freesound-based studies — where the album is now, before you.
          </p>
          <div class="listen">
            <SoundCloudPlayer
              url={OPEN_CALL.playlist}
              workTitle="What the C*rp*s"
              note="Preliminary studies built from Creative Commons material. The album proper is to be made from contributed recordings."
            />
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="prose prose--continued">
          <h2>What I am asking for</h2>
          <p>
            Recordings. They do not have to be instruments — and some of the best material is not.
            Objects, surfaces, metal, wood, glass, paper, water, voice, breath. Extended techniques
            are very welcome: bowing something that is not meant to be bowed, scraping, rubbing,
            preparing, striking, blowing across rather than into.
          </p>
          <p>
            You do not need to read music. Once you sign up I will send a page of graphic notation —
            short prompts, no staff, no traditional score — along with these recording notes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="brief">
          <h2 id="brief" class="section__title">Recording notes</h2>
          <p class="section__intro">
            A recording made at home is completely fine. These are not standards to live up to — they
            are the handful of things that decide whether a recording can be sliced into a corpus at
            all.
          </p>
          <dl class="brief">
            {
              BRIEF.map(([term, detail]) => (
                <div class="brief__row">
                  <dt class="brief__term">{term}</dt>
                  <dd class="brief__detail">{detail}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pipeline">
          <h2 id="pipeline" class="section__title">What happens to your recording</h2>
          <ol class="pipeline">
            {
              PIPELINE.map(([n, term, detail]) => (
                <li class="pipeline__step">
                  <span class="pipeline__index">{n}</span>
                  <span class="pipeline__body">
                    <span class="pipeline__term">{term}</span>
                    <span class="pipeline__detail">{detail}</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>
          <p class="section__intro pipeline__coda">
            Two rules fall out of steps 01 and 02, and they are the whole reason the notes above
            look the way they do: <strong>a slice with no attack never gets cut</strong>, and
            <strong>a slice with a chopped tail is measured as something it is not</strong>. Dry,
            spaced, and left to ring — everything else is taste.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rights">
          <h2 id="rights" class="section__title">Your rights</h2>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              <strong>You keep the copyright in your recording.</strong> You are not signing it over.
              You are giving me permission to use it, and that permission is not exclusive — the
              recording stays entirely yours to release, sell or reuse anywhere else.
            </p>
            <p>The form asks you to agree to exactly this:</p>
            <blockquote>
              The contributor retains copyright in the recording. The contributor grants Ömer
              Kurtuluş a worldwide, perpetual, royalty-free, non-exclusive licence to use, adapt,
              transform and recombine the recording within the corpus, and to publish the resulting
              works. The contributor may continue to use the recording for any purpose. The
              contributor is credited on the release.
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              Two things I want to be plain about rather than bury. <strong>The album is released
              under a Creative Commons licence</strong>, so the finished pieces can be reused by
              other people — not your recording, but what the system makes out of it. And the album
              may be sold, on Bandcamp and elsewhere. If either of those is not something you want
              your sound inside, this is the moment to say no, and no hard feelings.
            </p>
            <p>
              The licence is perpetual because a release cannot be unpicked after the fact. Your
              legal name is collected so the permission means something; only your credit name is
              published.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="submit">
          <h2 id="submit" class="section__title">Send a recording</h2>
          {
            tallySrc ? (
              <div class="form">
                <iframe
                  data-tally-src={tallySrc}
                  loading="lazy"
                  width="100%"
                  height="500"
                  title="Submission form"
                />
              </div>
            ) : (
              <div class="notice">
                <p class="notice__text">
                  The submission form opens shortly. Until then, write to me and I will send the
                  notation and the recording notes directly.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <a class="link link--strong" href={`mailto:${OPEN_CALL.contactEmail}`}>
                    {OPEN_CALL.contactEmail}
                  </a>
                </p>
              </div>
            )
          }
          <p class="form__note">
            Files larger than 200 MB will not upload — the form has a field for a Drive, Dropbox or
            WeTransfer link instead. Your name and email are used for this project only, are not
            shared with anyone, and I will delete them on request.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="faq">
          <h2 id="faq" class="section__title">Questions people ask</h2>
          <dl class="brief">
            {
              FAQ.map(([q, a]) => (
                <div class="brief__row">
                  <dt class="brief__term brief__term--q">{q}</dt>
                  <dd class="brief__detail">{a}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="contributors">
          <h2 id="contributors" class="section__title">Contributors</h2>
          {
            CONTRIBUTORS.length > 0 ? (
              <ul class="contributors">
                {CONTRIBUTORS.map((c) => (
                  <li class="contributors__item">
                    <span class="contributors__name">{c.name}</span>
                    {c.location && <span class="contributors__meta">{c.location}</span>}
                    {c.material && <span class="contributors__meta">{c.material}</span>}
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            ) : (
              <p class="section__intro">
                The list opens with the first submission. Everyone who sends a recording is named
                here and on the release.
              </p>
            )
          }
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="body__aside">
        {
          OPEN_CALL.deadline && (
            <section class="panel">
              <h2 class="eyebrow">Deadline</h2>
              <p class="panel__value">{OPEN_CALL.deadline}</p>
            </section>
          )
        }

        <section class="panel">
          <h2 class="eyebrow">In short</h2>
          <ul class="panel__list">
            <li>WAV, 44.1 or 48 kHz</li>
            <li>Dry — no processing</li>
            <li>Peak ≈ −6 dBFS</li>
            <li>30 s – 3 min per file</li>
            <li>Let the decay ring out</li>
            <li>You keep your copyright</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="panel">
          <h2 class="eyebrow">The system</h2>
          <p class="panel__text">
            Somax2, developed at IRCAM — a co-creative agent that listens continuously and navigates
            a corpus in real time.
          </p>
          <p class="panel__value">
            <a
              class="link"
              href="https://forum.ircam.fr/projects/detail/somax-2/"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              target="_blank">Somax2 at IRCAM →</a
            >
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel">
          <h2 class="eyebrow">For the curious</h2>
          <p class="panel__text">
            The Max patcher I use for this album, as it actually runs. It needs Max 9 and the Somax
            2.7 package installed; it will not open usefully without them.
          </p>
          <p class="panel__value">
            <a class="link link--strong" href={OPEN_CALL.patcher} download
              >Download the patcher →</a
            >
          </p>
          <p class="panel__text">
            Ask me anything about how it works. I would rather explain it than have it look like a
            trick.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="panel">
          <h2 class="eyebrow">Ask</h2>
          <p class="panel__value">
            <a class="link link--strong" href={`mailto:${OPEN_CALL.contactEmail}`}>
              {OPEN_CALL.contactEmail}
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {tallySrc && <script is:inline async src="https://tally.so/widgets/embed.js" />}
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .body {
    row-gap: var(--space-l);
    padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .body__main {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .body__aside {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    align-content: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .body__main {
      grid-column: 1 / 8;
    }
    .body__aside {
      grid-column: 9 / -1;
      /* Without align-self the grid item stretches to the row height, which
         leaves sticky no range to travel and the panels scroll away after one
         screen. */
      align-self: start;
      position: sticky;
      top: calc(var(--space-l) + 2rem);
    }
  }

  .section {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    padding-top: var(--space-s);
    border-top: var(--rule-thin) solid var(--color-line);
  }

  .section__title {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .section__intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* Term and detail sit in two columns on wide screens so the brief reads as a
     specification rather than as more prose. */
  .brief {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .brief__row + .brief__row {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    padding-top: var(--space-s);
    border-top: var(--rule-hair) solid var(--color-line);
  }

  .brief__term {
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .brief__term--q {
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: none;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .brief__detail {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  @media (min-width: 44rem) {
    .brief__row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 12ch 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-s);
      align-items: baseline;
    }
    .brief__row:has(.brief__term--q) {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0;
    }
    .brief__detail {
      margin-top: 0;
    }
  }

  .listen {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* A set needs room for its track list; the shared player sizes for one track. */
  .listen :global(iframe) {
    height: 320px;
  }

  .prose--continued {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
  }

  .pipeline {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-s);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .pipeline__step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 4ch 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-s);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .pipeline__index {
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .pipeline__body {
    display: block;
  }

  .pipeline__term {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .pipeline__detail {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .pipeline__coda {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .form {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .form :global(iframe) {
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  .form__note {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .notice {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    padding: var(--space-s);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: var(--rule-thick) solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .notice__text {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .contributors {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .contributors__item {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
    border-bottom: var(--rule-hair) solid var(--color-line);
  }

  .contributors__name {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .contributors__meta {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .panel {
    border-top: var(--rule-thin) solid var(--color-line);
    padding-top: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .panel__value {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .panel__text {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .panel__list {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
